From b11d411ea518120665a8125140c071aacd9193af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: D-Ogi <6542335+D-Ogi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:03:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] radv/wddm2: test runtime-bound queue contexts against a
 scripted host

The test drives the compiled radv_wddm2_cs.c and radv_wddm2_bo.c through
a host dispatch that hands out contexts, fences and allocations, records
every call with its thread and bind scope, and fails a chosen call on
request. It is built outside meson with the compile command of
radv_wddm2_cs.c; each test runs as its own process.

bind_failure: a failure of the context, the progress fence or the
companion fence leaves nothing behind, the context goes back through the
same cookie within the bind, the queue refuses to submit and binds on a
retry. A lost device, a second bind and an unbind of an unbound queue
are refused without a host call.

second_queue: two queues hold two contexts and four fences, each context
created through its own queue's cookie.

signal_wait: queue A signals a fence that queue B waits for on the GPU;
every call names only the context and progress fence of its queue, and
reusing a gather slot waits on the queue's own progress fence.

gather_teardown: unbind waits for the last submission, then releases the
progress fence, the gather BO, the companion fence and the context, in
that order, inside the unbind; afterwards the queue makes no host call
and the other queue still submits.

device_loss: unbind on a lost device makes no CPU wait, destroys nothing,
keeps the context and both fences and reports VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST; the
queue is not bound again, even once the device is back, and destroying
it makes no host call.

abandon: destroying a bound queue releases its fences and never calls
through the cookie.

outside_scope: a queue never bound refuses submissions and sparse
transactions without reaching the host, and no call arrives after the
last call returned.

internal_queue: the internal queue binds with a NULL cookie and outlives
an application queue.

sparse: a sparse bind on one queue uses only that queue's context and
companion fence.

rebind: a queue bound again for another cookie, after its first user
wrapped the gather slots, gets a new context and new fences and waits
for nothing of the first user.

review_wait_failure and review_destroy_failure, the reproducers of review
606 with their checks unchanged: a failed CPU wait destroys no fence and
keeps the progress fence; a failed operation 7 does not report success and
keeps the context handle. In both the queue is not bound again and its
destruction makes no host call.

The tests call the winsys hooks directly. They do not cover the instance
chain parser, the public bind and unbind entries with their queue lock or
the queue count admission of vkCreateDevice.

Every test also checks that queue-scoped calls happen only inside the
bind or unbind of their cookie, on the calling thread, and never name a
destroyed context or fence.
